// ============================================================================
// roulette-expected-value.epher -- red versus a single number in Europe
//
// A European roulette wheel has 37 pockets: 18 red, 18 black and one
// green zero. A $10 bet on red wins $10 with probability 18/37 and
// loses $10 with probability 19/37, and a $10 bet on a single number
// pays 35 to 1, winning $350 with probability 1/37 and losing $10 with
// probability 36/37:
//
//   E[red]          = 10 * 18/37 - 10 * 19/37 = -10/37
//   E[single number] = 350 * 1/37 - 10 * 36/37 = -10/37
//
// Both bets lose 10/37 of a dollar, about 27 cents, per $10 spin: the
// house edge is 1/37 of every dollar staked, about 2.7 percent, and it
// is the same for every bet on the wheel, which is the design.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/probability/games/roulette-expected-value.epher"
// Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
//
// The knob to edit is `stake`; everything below it is machinery.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the knob ----
const stake = 10

// ---- results ----
// Betting on red: win 18 of 37 pockets, lose the other 19.
print("E[$10 on red]:", stake * 18/37 - stake * 19/37)

// Betting on a single number: 35 times the stake on one pocket.
print("E[$10 on a single number]:", stake * 35 * (1/37) - stake * 36 * (1/37))

// The two expectations are the same exact fraction, -10/37 dollars.
print("exact: -10/37 dollars per spin")

// The house edge: the casino keeps 1/37 of every dollar staked, and in
// percent units that is exactly 100/37 = 2.7027..., regardless of the
// bet chosen.
print("house edge as a fraction of the stake:", 1/37)
print("house edge, percent of the stake:", 100 * (1/37))
// ---- expected output ----
// = 10
// = E[$10 on red]: -10/37
// = E[$10 on a single number]: -10/37
// = exact: -10/37 dollars per spin
// = house edge as a fraction of the stake: 1/37
// = house edge, percent of the stake: 100/37
